{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791594519,"nonce":"SDEUdHnT4uBxvDlBRxZWVU/Eljk=","title":"Inline text flow for HTML text elements","message":"A p or h1 holding a, strong, em, code and plain text should wrap as one paragraph on a shared baseline. Today each piece is a flex item, so the pieces sit in a row and only wrap between whole items (from the survey's probe of \"Hello \u003cstrong\u003ebold\u003c/strong\u003e long tail\"; not re-run here). The user-agent sheet sets text elements to a wrapping row, which is as close as flex gets.\n\nScope\n- Lay a text element's inline children out with layoutInline (already in blinc_abi and bound as Paragraph, which takes fixed runs and knows no elements).\n- Hits on any line piece go to the element that owns it; the flow redoes itself after layout settles; text-align including justify.\n- Wiki prose depends on it.\n\nReference, for structure: ashui's haxe/ashui/text/InlineFlow.hx (842 lines). The engine side (blinc_abi inline.rs) is ready.\n\nAcceptance: a paragraph with nested inline elements wraps at the container's width with each line on one baseline, and a click on a wrapped link piece reaches that link; pixel and hit tests at 1x and 2x.","files":null}]}